{"criteria": [
  {"id": "commits_to_a_specific_action_quickly", "verdict": "met", "justification": "The response opens with an unambiguous decision: 'I would order an immediate rollback of the new release (or disable it via feature flags, if applicable)' rather than laying out options to weigh."},
  {"id": "distinguishes_reversible_from_expensive_to_reverse", "verdict": "met", "justification": "The response explicitly frames the tradeoff as 'Announcements can be walked back; broken trust is harder to repair,' directly comparing the reversibility of the rollback/announcement delay against the harder-to-undo cost of leaving the bug live for days."},
  {"id": "weighs_ongoing_harm_against_launch_momentum", "verdict": "met", "justification": "The response weighs 'a several-day resolution timeline is unsustainable' and accumulating support/trust damage against the 'politically uncomfortable and embarrassing' cost of walking back the public announcement, concluding the former outweighs the latter."},
  {"id": "avoids_betting_on_unvalidated_fast_fix", "verdict": "met", "justification": "Rather than pushing engineering for a rushed patch, it explicitly instructs them to 'take the necessary several days to build and rigorously test the proper fix in a staging environment,' avoiding reliance on an unvalidated quick fix."},
  {"id": "proposes_concrete_next_step", "verdict": "met", "justification": "Beyond the rollback, it lays out customer/support communications, a staged re-launch message ('We expect to resume the rollout in X days'), and a blameless post-mortem as follow-on steps."}
]}
